# Kedom content

Pipeline: `docs/system/compendium-pipeline.md`. Schema: `docs/forge/schema.md`. Formats: `docs/forge/export-formats.md`. Localisation: `docs/forge/localisation.md`.

## Source of truth

`packages/content/content.sqlite` is the source of truth. Everything downstream is generated.

```
Forge UI -> content.sqlite -> dump.sql          (review)
                           -> packs/_source/**  (YAML, English, committed)
                           -> packs/*/          (LevelDB, gitignored)
                           -> lang/babele/ru/** (Babele JSON, committed)
                           -> site/v1/{en,ru}/** (public site, designed not built)
```

The public website and the Babele overlay are additional consumers, not second sources. Same invariants: slugs are identity across all of them. Design in `docs/forge/public-site-export.md` and `docs/forge/localisation.md`.

**Do not hand-edit `packages/system/packs/_source/`.** It is generated. Edit in Forge and re-export; the next export overwrites anything you change.

**Do not hand-edit `packages/system/lang/babele/`.** Same pipeline, different target. Russian content overlays.

**Do not hand-edit `packages/content/dump.sql`.** Regenerate with `pnpm forge:dump`.

## One-way, always

Export flows outward and never back. `pnpm packs:extract` exists only as a diagnostic — to see what Foundry actually stored. **Its output is never an input.**

Reversing this creates two sources of truth for one record, which means building either a diff UI or arbitrary conflict rules. Neither is worth it for a single-author project.

Content edited inside a Foundry world is play state, not source, and is deliberately not captured.

## Identity

| Field | Mutable | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | never | SQLite primary key, internal to Forge |
| `slug` | never | cross-system identity, referenced by system code and exports |
| `foundry_id` | never | the Foundry document `_id`; regenerating it breaks every reference in every world |
| `label` | freely | English display name. Russian is a `translation` row, never a second column. |

**Never derive meaning from a label.** No name parsing, ever. CoC7, WFRP4e, and SWFFG all encode specialisation identity in the display name (`"Locksmith (Craft)"`) and parse it back; WFRP4e additionally stores career skill lists as name strings, so a rename needs a routine to rewrite every referencing career, with silent breakage on any missed path. See `docs/research/04-skill-systems.md`.

Slug format: lower-case, `[a-z0-9]`, dot-separated segments, nesting under the owner — `survive.tracking`, `survive.environment.forest`.

## Localisation

English is canonical. Russian is an overlay. Design: `docs/forge/localisation.md`.

- Entity tables hold English `label` / `description`. The `translation` table holds `locale='ru'` only.
- Slugs are never translated.
- YAML packs are English. Russian content ships as generated Babele JSON.
- `lang/{en,ru}.json` are **source** (UI strings). `lang/babele/` is **generated** (content).
- Missing Russian is a fallback to English, not an export error. Never copy English into a Russian field to fake completeness.
- A third locale is new rows, not new columns.

## Determinism

Same database in, same bytes out. Otherwise every export is a diff full of noise and the reviewability that justifies the YAML stage disappears.

- Keys sorted, indentation fixed, UTF-8, LF endings.
- **No timestamps** in generated output.
- `dump.sql` rows ordered by primary key, schema statements first.
- Filenames are the slug: `survive.yml`.
- One file per document, not one per pack, so a content change touches one file.

## UTF-8

Kedom names carry diacritics from a constructed language — `Kedôm`, `Nitól`, `Ñiziel` — and Russian content is Cyrillic. No escaping, no Unicode normalisation, no transliteration on the way out.

## Validate before exporting

Refuse to export invalid content rather than emitting something Foundry chokes on, and report **every** problem at once. The full check list is in `docs/forge/export-formats.md`; the one that matters most is that a race cannot grant a skill that does not exist — which would have caught Q10 in `docs/rules/99-open-questions.md` at authoring time instead of at play time.

## Reproducibility

The chain must run from a fresh clone. `content.sqlite` and `dump.sql` are both committed for this reason — the database because it is the source, the dump because a binary file tells a reviewer nothing and is the recovery path.

`foundryvtt-wwn` is the counter-example: all fourteen of its pack-generation scripts point at a gitignored directory, so nobody but its author can rebuild its packs.
